WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.209 --> 00:00:02.261
- There's donut day, there's pizza day.

00:00:02.261 --> 00:00:05.557
- 2013 was the International
Year of Quinoa.

00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:06.390
- What?

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.223
- And I love quinoa.

00:00:07.223 --> 00:00:08.056
- I BB too!

00:00:08.056 --> 00:00:08.889
- A whole year?

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:10.248
How do you even celebrate that?

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:13.025
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.025 --> 00:00:15.146
- I've always been
inspired by regular people

00:00:15.146 --> 00:00:17.595
who decide to follow their passion,

00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:20.446
and then doing so, end up
having a positive impact

00:00:20.446 --> 00:00:23.344
on the world, bigger than
they could've ever imagined.

00:00:23.344 --> 00:00:26.637
One of those people is
my friend Hank Fortener.

00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:28.168
You started a holiday.

00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:29.042
- Yes.
(laughs)

00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:30.250
- Tell me about that.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.898
- It actually came about
only because it didn't exist.

00:00:32.898 --> 00:00:35.288
I think, sometimes, making an
impact feels a lot for people

00:00:35.288 --> 00:00:37.778
like, "I wanna be a part of
that, I wanna be part of that,

00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:38.665
"I wanna be part of that."

00:00:38.665 --> 00:00:41.528
You can't stand out just by looking

00:00:41.528 --> 00:00:43.418
where nobody's paying attention to it,

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:45.546
what does everybody thing
is hard at being done.

00:00:45.546 --> 00:00:48.137
I had a big personal
experience with adoption.

00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:51.217
After three of us biologically,
my parents fostered 36 kids,

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.636
over a seven-year period.

00:00:52.636 --> 00:00:55.525
We had an amazing family
unit, that focused on

00:00:55.525 --> 00:00:57.116
taking care of kids that
didn't have a family.

00:00:57.116 --> 00:00:59.805
Family never had anything to
do with last names or blood,

00:00:59.805 --> 00:01:04.205
or color, or DNA, it was
always who was in our home,

00:01:04.205 --> 00:01:06.205
that we were taking care
of, that's who family was.

00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:08.293
We kinda slammed our face into the system,

00:01:08.293 --> 00:01:10.485
which is not serving kids well.

00:01:10.485 --> 00:01:13.765
I had a little brother for
three years, his name is Robbie,

00:01:13.765 --> 00:01:16.101
and just an unbelievable,
unbelievable kid,

00:01:16.101 --> 00:01:17.843
and we bonded super super fast,

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:19.533
he was with us for three years.

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:22.165
Then, we got a phone
call and within 24 hours,

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:24.723
the police and a social worker came

00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:26.213
to take him out of our home.

00:01:26.213 --> 00:01:28.285
So, take a six-year old,
three-year old, four-year old,

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:29.344
into the system,

00:01:29.344 --> 00:01:31.313
send them to different sets
of parents and families.

00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:33.605
You don't remember every
house, you just know,

00:01:33.605 --> 00:01:36.005
"The world is gonna use
me and pass me around."

00:01:36.005 --> 00:01:38.405
They turn 18, they're handed
trash bags to hold their items.

00:01:38.405 --> 00:01:40.264
Most of the times, sometimes
it's great programs,

00:01:40.264 --> 00:01:43.213
the YMCA and YWCA created
some really great programs,

00:01:43.213 --> 00:01:44.954
but for a minority of these kids.

00:01:44.954 --> 00:01:46.372
Most of them end up unemployed,

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.573
most of them end up in prison.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:50.773
That part of the journey
was super difficult for me,

00:01:50.773 --> 00:01:52.403
and which is part of the
reason what shifted our family

00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:53.605
into a permanent adoption.

00:01:53.605 --> 00:01:55.005
So, we had seven years of foster care,

00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:57.645
and they pursued to do
10 years of adoption,

00:01:57.645 --> 00:02:00.053
and we adopted eight kids
from six different countries.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.242
- Wow.

00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:02.704
So, we see where your drive

00:02:02.704 --> 00:02:04.592
for adoption comes from.
- For sure.

00:02:04.592 --> 00:02:07.272
- And then, from that,
you started a organization

00:02:07.272 --> 00:02:08.330
called AdoptTogether.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.163
- Yeah.

00:02:09.163 --> 00:02:11.042
AdoptTogether was started as a platform,

00:02:11.042 --> 00:02:12.952
that helps families
fundraise for their adoption.

00:02:12.952 --> 00:02:13.785
- Okay.

00:02:13.785 --> 00:02:15.202
- When we realized it's so expensive,

00:02:15.202 --> 00:02:18.322
it could be towards $40,000,
$50,000 to adopt domestically.

00:02:18.322 --> 00:02:19.904
If you're a teacher or a cop,

00:02:19.904 --> 00:02:21.602
if you make 50 or 60 grand
a year, they're like,

00:02:21.602 --> 00:02:23.573
"I'll take a year's salary in cash."

00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:25.442
You know, what's the percentage
of people that have that?

00:02:25.442 --> 00:02:26.275
- Yeah.

00:02:26.275 --> 00:02:28.080
- 86% of people who considered adoption,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:28.972
they just bail.

00:02:28.972 --> 00:02:31.032
So, we hope that people might
look at the price tag and go,

00:02:31.032 --> 00:02:32.180
"It's a sign I shouldn't adopt."

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.320
But they look at AdoptTogether and go,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.852
"Okay, it's a sign we should adopt."

00:02:34.852 --> 00:02:36.692
- And from what point, after
starting your foundation,

00:02:36.692 --> 00:02:37.532
did you realize,

00:02:37.532 --> 00:02:39.612
"Hey, let's turn this
thing into a holiday?"

00:02:39.612 --> 00:02:41.900
- I'm a friend of Scott Harrison,
who started Charity Water.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.942
Been an amazing support, and
also a mentor in the process.

00:02:44.942 --> 00:02:46.553
And every time I would tell
him about what I was doing,

00:02:46.553 --> 00:02:48.182
he would always say, "Throw a party."

00:02:48.182 --> 00:02:51.533
As it turns out, you need
basically cool parties

00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:52.933
to raise money in the world.

00:02:52.933 --> 00:02:54.979
So, we're about to hit our $5 million,

00:02:54.979 --> 00:02:56.293
just a massive success,

00:02:56.293 --> 00:02:58.840
for us, that was like way
beyond what we'd imagined.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.992
So, we started that in
2012, and then, in 2013,

00:03:02.992 --> 00:03:05.621
we were like, "We gotta
figure out how to celebrate.

00:03:05.621 --> 00:03:06.541
"We gotta throw a party."

00:03:06.541 --> 00:03:08.672
We'll do something around
World Adoption Day.

00:03:08.672 --> 00:03:10.430
And we're sitting in a room,
talking about the party,

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.590
and I finally said, "Hey
guys, somebody Google

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:13.893
"when World Adoption Day is,

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.293
or we'll just do our own adoption day,

00:03:15.293 --> 00:03:16.370
'cause it has to be a World Adoption Day."

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:17.491
- There has to exist, right?

00:03:17.491 --> 00:03:18.760
- They're sitting there, "Can't find it."

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.421
I'm like, "Okay, could
I have the computer?

00:03:20.421 --> 00:03:21.651
"Let me show you how the internet works."

00:03:21.651 --> 00:03:22.484
(laughs)

00:03:22.484 --> 00:03:24.809
And I'm looking, and I
make a fool of this person,

00:03:24.809 --> 00:03:27.400
and then I'm standing there
and the pressure is cooking,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.789
'cause I'm like, "I could
not find World Adoption Day."

00:03:29.789 --> 00:03:31.678
And I was like, "We know what to do now.

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:34.051
"We're gonna create World Adoption Day."

00:03:34.051 --> 00:03:35.051
And then, I looked at the guys,

00:03:35.051 --> 00:03:36.800
"Find out if the domain is available,"

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.040
which, of course, there's no such thing,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.701
so yes, the domain was available.

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:42.061
So, we started the domain, got
one of the guys at the table,

00:03:42.061 --> 00:03:43.640
"Hey, I'm gonna build a site."

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.901
We built the site that
night, drew everything up,

00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:47.720
rocked it and just said,
"Alright, we gotta do it."

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.149
I started calling people and saying,

00:03:49.149 --> 00:03:52.149
"Hey, we're gonna create
World Adoption Day,"

00:03:52.149 --> 00:03:53.421
and one of the guys was,

00:03:53.421 --> 00:03:55.840
"I think you should
touch base with the UN,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.410
because they sort of do that."

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:58.243
- Yeah.

00:03:58.243 --> 00:04:00.429
- And I was, "Definitely,
I should do that."

00:04:00.429 --> 00:04:01.629
- That was on my list.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:03.301
- So, I called the UN and I just said,

00:04:03.301 --> 00:04:06.179
"Hey, I'd like to submit a day
for international observance,

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:07.080
which I was super proud of myself

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.171
for knowing how to say that statement,

00:04:09.171 --> 00:04:11.021
and he said, "Are you an ambassador?"

00:04:11.021 --> 00:04:11.999
"You better believe it.

00:04:11.999 --> 00:04:14.311
I'm an ambassador for World Adoption Day."

00:04:14.311 --> 00:04:15.929
And he's like, "No, are you a delegate?"

00:04:15.929 --> 00:04:17.791
"Yes, I have delegated myself

00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:19.317
to make this phone call to you,"

00:04:19.317 --> 00:04:20.839
and the guy's like, "That's
not how this works."

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:24.923
He gives me this line
about how hard it is,

00:04:24.923 --> 00:04:26.521
and how long it takes, and
you gotta meet these people.

00:04:26.521 --> 00:04:28.472
I go, "Cool, then I'm just gonna do it,

00:04:28.472 --> 00:04:29.779
"'cause we've already picked a day,

00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:31.601
"which I was gonna tell you
about, it's November 9th."

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:33.891
He's like, "Okay, yeah, if you can do it,

00:04:33.891 --> 00:04:35.592
"but it won't be real."

00:04:35.592 --> 00:04:37.752
Then I was, "No offense, but this is the

00:04:37.752 --> 00:04:39.141
International Year of Quinoa,

00:04:39.141 --> 00:04:41.941
and I'm the only person
that I know who knows that."

00:04:41.941 --> 00:04:42.774
- Yeah (laughs).

00:04:42.774 --> 00:04:44.631
- "So, if I can make it
real, then you and I work out

00:04:44.631 --> 00:04:45.928
the logistics later," and he's like,

00:04:45.928 --> 00:04:47.363
"Fine. Good luck."

00:04:47.363 --> 00:04:49.083
So, we needed some activation,

00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:52.323
we needed some way to
communicate to people,

00:04:52.323 --> 00:04:54.952
they were celebrating, give
people a way to celebrate.

00:04:54.952 --> 00:04:58.712
A lot of ideas came up and
I love a lot of art history,

00:04:58.712 --> 00:05:00.128
and how art has impacted culture.

00:05:00.128 --> 00:05:02.851
And there's a story of
a guy named Harvey Ball.

00:05:02.851 --> 00:05:05.891
Harvey was an artist 50 years ago,

00:05:05.891 --> 00:05:07.421
who created the Smiley Face.

00:05:07.421 --> 00:05:09.432
And I remember that story
and remember those pieces,

00:05:09.432 --> 00:05:11.971
when somebody said, "We need
a visual, we need an icon,

00:05:11.971 --> 00:05:15.563
"we need what is World Adoption Day."

00:05:15.563 --> 00:05:17.805
For me, having come from
a world where adoption

00:05:17.805 --> 00:05:21.140
was such a tragic story to begin with,

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.512
you don't come to adoption

00:05:22.512 --> 00:05:24.763
because everything is going smoothly.

00:05:24.763 --> 00:05:25.596
- Yeah.

00:05:25.596 --> 00:05:27.569
- You come to adoption
because a birth mother

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:30.981
tragically is either not
well or not capable or able

00:05:30.981 --> 00:05:32.665
of her own volitioncess,

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.191
"I know I cannot give this child life."

00:05:35.191 --> 00:05:38.619
So a child and their mother are separated,

00:05:38.619 --> 00:05:42.460
that's where adoption
starts, which is tragic.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.285
What adoption does is
redeems that broken feeling,

00:05:45.285 --> 00:05:48.935
it brings a healing and
hope, joy and a celebration.

00:05:48.935 --> 00:05:53.833
So I said, "Let's draw a
smiley face on people's hands,

00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:56.294
"and have them post a selfie and say,

00:05:56.294 --> 00:05:57.512
Happy World Adoption Day."

00:05:57.512 --> 00:05:58.654
I wanted to raise-

00:05:58.654 --> 00:06:00.603
- I love that, it makes you feel good,

00:06:00.603 --> 00:06:02.065
as soon as you see it, you're smiling.

00:06:02.065 --> 00:06:04.633
- Yeah, everything can suck around you,

00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:06.421
but if for one day, everybody's gonna say,

00:06:06.421 --> 00:06:10.023
"Hey, family is everything,
every child deserves a family,"

00:06:10.023 --> 00:06:12.205
and we're gonna boost morale
around this conversation

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:14.965
that has, otherwise, been
taboo or awkward or uneasy,

00:06:14.965 --> 00:06:16.773
and you get to celebrate people.

00:06:16.773 --> 00:06:19.770
We got Shaq to post, so it
got people pumped and excited,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.623
since this we've had
Ellen and Charlize Theron,

00:06:21.623 --> 00:06:25.234
and so many people whose
lives have been touched,

00:06:25.234 --> 00:06:27.143
and they posted a smiley face.

00:06:27.143 --> 00:06:30.893
It's 100% participation,
anybody can participate

00:06:30.893 --> 00:06:34.605
and vote for families and
vote for kids to have a home.

00:06:34.605 --> 00:06:37.613
I wanted to change the way
people imagined family,

00:06:37.613 --> 00:06:39.714
and the amount of people who posted this,

00:06:39.714 --> 00:06:42.314
who said, "Hey, I'm adopted,
I've never told my story,

00:06:42.314 --> 00:06:44.861
"I've never told my family
how grateful I am for them."

00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:46.333
People posted and said,
"I have a little brother

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:48.860
"who's adopted, I've
never told people that."

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:50.941
It's just extraordinary for me to go,

00:06:50.941 --> 00:06:53.511
even though all these stories
begin with tragedy somewhere.

00:06:53.511 --> 00:06:54.344
- Yeah.

00:06:54.344 --> 00:06:56.059
- For one day, we're
just gonna celebrate that

00:06:56.059 --> 00:06:57.951
because of those tragedies.

00:06:57.951 --> 00:07:02.650
I have my brother Brendon, and
Matthew, and Hope, and Gab.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.538
I have these people in my
life because of that tragedy,

00:07:05.538 --> 00:07:07.338
and for one day, I'm gonna celebrate it.

00:07:07.338 --> 00:07:08.857
- You basically started a movement.

00:07:08.857 --> 00:07:12.120
- Hopefully. (laughs) Hopefully.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.953
(inspiring music)

